  "raw": " catch up, doubts are growing whether the Galaxy Note 7 can be salvaged.\n\nThe company has recalled more than 1.5 million Galaxy Note 7 handsets worldwide, amid questions over the device's exploding batteries. LG has publicly suggested it'll stop selling the phablet, and its new Galaxy Tab S2 is looking stronger after early technical problems.\n\nThe U.K. last week announced that it's drying up on Galaxy Note 7s. Last week's recall by the European Union came shortly after several high-profile retailers including Carphone Warehouse, O2, and EE stopped welcoming the devices. Carphone even suggested customers buy other devices with \"back-up\" batteries to take their place.\n\nThe excitement around the Galaxy Note 7 has turned into an embarrassment for Samsung and could pose an existential threat to the company as it enters a second decade.\n\nWho will buy the Galaxy Tab S2?\n\nSamsung needs the success of the Galaxy Note 7 to counter",
  "serialized": "catch up, doubts are growing whether the Galaxy Note 7 can be salvaged. The company has recalled more than 1.5 million Galaxy Note 7 handsets worldwide, amid questions over the device's exploding batteries. LG has publicly suggested it'll stop selling the phablet, and its new Galaxy Tab S2 is looking stronger after early technical problems. The U.K. last week announced that it's drying up on Galaxy Note 7s. Last week's recall by the European Union came shortly after several high-profile retailers including Carphone Warehouse, O2, and EE stopped welcoming the devices. Carphone even suggested customers buy other devices with \"back-up\" batteries to take their place. The excitement around the Galaxy Note 7 has turned into an embarrassment for Samsung and could pose an existential threat to the company as it enters a second decade. Who will buy the Galaxy Tab S2? Samsung needs the success of the Galaxy Note 7 to counter",
